  const P = [
    { id: 'p1', level: 'A2', title: 'Awa’s Day', text: 'Awa gets up at six o’clock every morning. She washes her face, eats bread with tea and walks to school with her brother. Classes start at eight. At noon she eats rice with her friends under a big tree. In the afternoon she has English and science. After school she helps her mother in the garden. She goes to bed at nine.', qs: [Q('What time does Awa get up?', ['At six', 'At eight', 'At nine', 'At noon'], 0, 'Elle se lève à six heures.'), Q('Where does Awa eat lunch?', ['Under a big tree', 'At home', 'In the library', 'At the market'], 0, 'Elle déjeune sous un grand arbre.'), Q('What does she do after school?', ['She helps her mother in the garden.', 'She plays football.', 'She watches television.', 'She goes to the market.'], 0, 'Elle aide sa mère au jardin.')] },
    { id: 'p2', level: 'A2', title: 'The Market in Bamako', text: 'The market in Bamako opens early. Fatoumata sells mangoes and oranges, and her prices are low. Customers come from all over the city. On Saturdays the market is very crowded, so many people arrive before seven. Fatoumata says: “I like my work because I meet many people, but my feet are tired in the evening.”', qs: [Q('What does Fatoumata sell?', ['Mangoes and oranges', 'Rice and fish', 'Shoes', 'Books'], 0, 'Elle vend des mangues et des oranges.'), Q('Why do many people arrive before seven on Saturdays?', ['The market is very crowded.', 'The market is closed.', 'Prices are high.', 'It is a holiday.'], 0, 'Le marché est bondé le samedi.'), Q('What is the problem with her work?', ['Her feet are tired in the evening.', 'Her prices are high.', 'She meets nobody.', 'She cannot count.'], 0, 'Elle a mal aux pieds le soir.')] },
    { id: 'p3', level: 'B1', title: 'Solar Panels in the Village', text: 'Two years ago, the village of Sanankoroba had no electricity. Then an engineer named Ibrahim installed twenty solar panels on the school roof. Today the school has light in the evening, so students can study after dark. The village also charges phones there for a small fee. However, the batteries are expensive, and the villagers worry about replacing them in five years.', qs: [Q('What did Ibrahim install?', ['Solar panels', 'A well', 'A road', 'A school'], 0, 'Il a installé des panneaux solaires.'), Q('Why can students study after dark?', ['The school has electric light.', 'They use candles.', 'The school is open all night.', 'They have new books.'], 0, 'L’école a de la lumière électrique.'), Q('What worries the villagers?', ['Replacing expensive batteries', 'Losing the panels', 'The price of phones', 'Finding an engineer'], 0, 'Remplacer les batteries coûteuses.')] },
    { id: 'p4', level: 'B1', title: 'A Young Entrepreneur', text: 'Mariam was seventeen when she started selling homemade soap. At first she sold only to neighbours, but her friends told others about her product. After six months she had thirty regular customers. She used her profits to buy better ingredients, and her soap became even more popular. Her biggest challenge, she says, was learning to manage money. “If I had known about budgeting earlier, I would have saved more,” she admits.', qs: [Q('What did Mariam sell?', ['Homemade soap', 'Food', 'Clothes', 'Phones'], 0, 'Du savon artisanal.'), Q('How many regular customers did she have after six months?', ['Thirty', 'Six', 'Seventeen', 'Ten'], 0, 'Trente clients réguliers.'), Q('What was her biggest challenge?', ['Managing money', 'Finding customers', 'Making soap', 'Going to school'], 0, 'Apprendre à gérer l’argent.')] },
    { id: 'p5', level: 'B1', title: 'The River Niger', text: 'The Niger is the third-longest river in Africa. It starts in Guinea, flows through Mali, Niger and Nigeria, and ends in the Atlantic Ocean. Millions of people depend on it for fishing, farming and transport. In Mali the river floods every year, which makes the soil fertile. Unfortunately, pollution and sand are now threatening parts of the river.', qs: [Q('Where does the Niger start?', ['In Guinea', 'In Mali', 'In Nigeria', 'In Niger'], 0, 'Il prend sa source en Guinée.'), Q('Why is the soil fertile in Mali?', ['The river floods every year.', 'It rains daily.', 'Farmers use machines.', 'The sand is rich.'], 0, 'Les crues annuelles enrichissent le sol.'), Q('What is threatening the river?', ['Pollution and sand', 'Fishing only', 'Too many boats', 'Too much rain'], 0, 'La pollution et le sable.')] },
    { id: 'p6', level: 'B2', title: 'The Brain Drain', text: 'Every year thousands of well-educated young Africans leave their countries to work abroad. This phenomenon, known as the brain drain, deprives their home countries of doctors, engineers and teachers. Supporters of emigration argue that migrants send money home, which helps their families. Critics reply that remittances cannot replace skilled workers. Some governments now offer incentives to encourage graduates to return.', qs: [Q('What is the “brain drain”?', ['Educated people leaving their country', 'A health problem', 'A kind of exam', 'A new technology'], 0, 'Le départ des personnes diplômées.'), Q('What do supporters of emigration say?', ['Migrants send money home.', 'Migrants never return.', 'Governments lose power.', 'Schools are closed.'], 0, 'Les migrants envoient de l’argent.'), Q('What do some governments do to bring graduates back?', ['They offer incentives.', 'They forbid travel.', 'They close borders.', 'They reduce salaries.'], 0, 'Elles offrent des incitations.')] },
    { id: 'p7', level: 'B2', title: 'Gold and Water', text: 'Artisanal gold mining provides income to thousands of families in West Africa. Nevertheless, miners often use mercury to separate gold from rock, and this toxic metal ends up in rivers. Fish absorb the mercury, and people who eat them may suffer serious health problems. Several organisations are now training miners to use safer methods, although change is slow because mercury is cheap and quick.', qs: [Q('Why do miners use mercury?', ['To separate gold from rock', 'To clean water', 'To feed fish', 'To build tools'], 0, 'Pour séparer l’or de la roche.'), Q('How can mercury affect people?', ['Through the fish they eat', 'Through the air only', 'Through gold jewellery', 'Through new tools'], 0, 'En mangeant des poissons contaminés.'), Q('Why is change slow?', ['Mercury is cheap and quick.', 'Miners refuse to work.', 'Training is forbidden.', 'There is no gold.'], 0, 'Le mercure est bon marché et rapide.')] },
    { id: 'p8', level: 'B2', title: 'Girls’ Education', text: 'Research shows that each additional year of schooling increases a girl’s future income and reduces early marriage. Educated mothers are also more likely to send their own children to school. Despite this, in many regions girls are still withdrawn from class to help at home or because fees are too high. Campaigns that provide scholarships and safe transport have raised attendance in several districts.', qs: [Q('What happens with each additional year of schooling?', ['A girl’s future income increases.', 'Fees decrease.', 'Marriages increase.', 'Schools close.'], 0, 'Le revenu futur augmente.'), Q('Why are some girls withdrawn from school?', ['To help at home or because fees are high', 'Because they refuse', 'Because teachers are absent', 'Because there are no books'], 0, 'Pour aider à la maison ou à cause des frais.'), Q('What helped to raise attendance?', ['Scholarships and safe transport', 'More homework', 'Longer holidays', 'Bigger classes'], 0, 'Bourses et transport sûr.')] },
    { id: 'p9', level: 'C1', title: 'The Ethics of Automation', text: 'Automation is widely hailed as a driver of productivity, yet its social consequences remain contentious. Whereas optimists contend that new technologies will create more jobs than they destroy, sceptics warn that the transition may be painful for workers whose skills become obsolete. Policymakers therefore face a dilemma: how to encourage innovation without exacerbating inequality. Many economists advocate retraining programmes, while others call for a universal basic income.', qs: [Q('What do optimists believe about automation?', ['It will create more jobs than it destroys.', 'It will end all work.', 'It is harmful.', 'It is too expensive.'], 0, 'Ils pensent que les emplois créés dépasseront ceux détruits.'), Q('What is the policymakers’ dilemma?', ['Encouraging innovation without increasing inequality', 'Choosing a language', 'Raising taxes only', 'Stopping technology'], 0, 'Innover sans aggraver les inégalités.'), Q('Which solutions do economists mention?', ['Retraining programmes and basic income', 'Longer working hours', 'Banning machines', 'Cutting wages'], 0, 'Formation et revenu de base.')] },
    { id: 'p10', level: 'C1', title: 'Language and Identity', text: 'Language is far more than a tool for communication; it shapes how communities perceive themselves. When a language disappears, an entire body of knowledge — proverbs, oral histories, ecological wisdom — may vanish with it. Linguists estimate that roughly half of the world’s languages could be extinct by the end of the century. Nonetheless, digital tools and community radio have enabled some endangered languages to experience a remarkable revival.', qs: [Q('According to the text, what disappears with a language?', ['A body of knowledge', 'Only vocabulary', 'Only grammar', 'Nothing important'], 0, 'Tout un savoir : proverbes, histoires, savoirs écologiques.'), Q('What do linguists estimate?', ['Half of the languages could disappear.', 'All languages will survive.', 'English will disappear.', 'Languages never change.'], 0, 'Environ la moitié pourrait disparaître.'), Q('What has helped some endangered languages?', ['Digital tools and community radio', 'Printed dictionaries only', 'Foreign schools', 'Laws against English'], 0, 'Outils numériques et radios communautaires.')] }
  ];
  window.EA_WORLD = { cases, traps: T, riddles: R, speaking: S, events: E, passages: P };
